Step 1: Log in on the serial SetUp port as an Administrator-level account
        (factory default: super / super) and reach the Text Interface command
        prompt. Keep this serial session open until Step 8 succeeds
        (and, if you add the final DROP, until Step 11 is done).

Step 2: Open the RADIUS Parameters menu
        /N [Enter]              Network Parameters (eth0/IPv4 [Shared])
        29 [Enter]              RADIUS Parameters -- 29 in WTI's user guide; the
                                item number varies by model/firmware (the TACACS
                                menu is 28 on the unit tacctl was tested with);
                                pick the entry labelled "RADIUS"

Step 3: Set each item (type the number the menu shows next to the label,
        [Enter], then the value -- WTI does not document the numbers here)
          Enable                        : On
          Primary Host/Address (IPv4)   : ${SERVER_IP}
          Primary Secret Word           : ${SECRET}
          Secondary Host/Address        : (leave Undefined -- single tacctl server)
          Fallback Timer                : 3   (factory default: seconds the primary is tried)
          Fallback Local                : ${FALLBACK_LOCAL}
          Retries                       : 3   (factory default)
          Authentication Port           : ${AUTH_PORT}
          Accounting Port               : ${ACCT_PORT}
          OneTime Auth                  : Off
          Default RADIUS User Access    : Enable On, Access Level ViewOnly,
                                          Service Access Telnet/SSH On   (see notes),
                                          Port Access: the serial ports User- and
                                          ViewOnly-level logins may reach (factory: none;
                                          see "Port access" below); on a power unit also
                                          Plug Access and Plug Group Access
          Debug                         : Off (turn On only while troubleshooting)
        Leave Session Module Type as it is: WTI does not document what it
        sends, and nothing on the server depends on it.

Step 4: Ping test
          Ping RADIUS Servers           -- confirms ${SERVER_IP} answers ICMP from the unit.
                                           ICMP only: a pass does NOT prove UDP ${AUTH_PORT} works (Step 5)

Step 5: Check the unit's firewall (IP Tables under /N)
        If the IP Tables list sets 'iptables -P INPUT DROP' or ends in
        'iptables -A INPUT -j DROP', it must also accept loopback and the
        replies to the unit's own requests, BEFORE the final DROP:
          iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT
          iptables -A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
        (older builds: -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED). The server
        answers over UDP from ${SERVER_IP} port ${AUTH_PORT} (and ${ACCT_PORT}).
        Without the rule the unit's requests leave, the answers are dropped,
        and every login waits out the Fallback Timer while the server's auth
        log shows it answered.

${IPTABLES_BLOCK}

Step 6: SNMP (so tacctl can read the unit's name and nothing else answers)
${SNMP_BLOCK}

Step 7: Save
        Press [Esc] repeatedly until the unit prints "Saving Configuration"
        and returns to the command prompt.

Step 8: Test from a SECOND session -- keep the Step 1 serial session open as a
        back door until this works. Log in with a user that is a member of
        scope '${SCOPE}', forcing the password method first:
          ssh -o PreferredAuthentications=password <user>@<wti-ip>
        then type /H: the Help menu lists only the commands allowed for the
        access level the server assigned (see the mapping below). Log out with
        /X. On the server, 'tacctl log tail 20 --backend radius' shows the
        attempt (see "Verify" below).
        If a plain 'ssh <user>@<wti-ip>' is closed without a password prompt
        while the password method works, the unit's Invalid Access Lockout is
        armed from earlier failures: /UL clears it.

Step 9: Only if Step 8 fails -- turn on the unit's RADIUS debug
        /N, 29, then Debug: On, [Esc] to save. Retry the Step 8 login and read
        what the unit logs next to the server's auth log (see "Verify"
        below), then set Debug back to Off and [Esc] to save.

Step 10: Break-glass local accounts (optional, per scope)
${BREAKGLASS_BLOCK}

Step 11: The final DROP of the IP Tables list (optional; the last step, pasted by you)
${IPTABLES_DROP_BLOCK}
